    "narrative": "Our strongest plays today are headlined by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (73.58) and **Bryce Harper** (72.35), both of whom offer the optimal intersection of high-tier talent and favorable matchup context. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a rare combination of an advantageous park factor and a slow opposing delivery that significantly enhances his stolen base probability, while **Bryce Harper** enters with a sharp BvP edge (60 PA) and excellent batted-ball metrics against his opponent, justifying his high-value UTIL spot. **Jonathan Aranda** (70.46) also grades out as an elite start, taking advantage of a highly favorable matchup against a pitcher with an xERA over 5.00, which effectively offsets his slight park disadvantage.\n\nOn the margins, the decision to bench **Gleyber Torres** (43.88) was the most difficult, as he hovered just above our threshold for consideration but ultimately lost out to higher-ceiling options like **Cal Raleigh** (66.98) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (65.48), whose dynamic platoon edges against weaker arms provided a safer floor. **Luis Arraez** (45.46) remains in the lineup despite the tough matchup against a sub-3.00 xERA arm, as his contact-heavy profile is less sensitive to opposing pitcher skill than our other power-oriented bats. However, users should monitor the status of **Masyn Winn** (54.55) and **Dylan Beavers** (60.22); both face pitchers who excel at holding runners, which suppresses their scoring ceiling given our specific focus on SB scarcity. While the remaining outfield contingent\u2014including **Ryan Waldschmidt**, **Taylor Ward**, **Daulton Varsho**, and **Gavin Sheets**\u2014all sit in the 52\u201357 range, they are effectively locking in our roster depth while we wait for more volatility in the projected starters for later in the week.",
